<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mont Cenis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Col du Mont Cenis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mont Cenis is a pass at about 2,080 metres in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a> valley in Savoy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. It was one of the main routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, controlled by the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and later the house of Savoy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> built a road over it between 1803 and 1810.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mont-cenis.jpg" alt="Mont Cenis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunther Hissler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Col_du_Mont_Cenis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mont Cenis, Moncenisio in Italian, is also the name of the massif around the pass, which rises to 3,612 metres at the Pointe de Ronce and marks the limit between the Cottian and Graian Alps. The name may come from mont des cendres, mountain of ashes, after a tradition of a forest fire that left the ground deep in ash. From Modane the carriage road climbs the Arc valley for 25.7 km to Lanslebourg, reaches the hospice a little beyond the summit, and descends through the Cenis valley to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, from where the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dora-riparia">Dora Riparia</a> leads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> came down into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> this way to fight Maxentius, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> crossed with his army to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> in 773; some authors believe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> used it too. Medieval pilgrims went over it to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-francigena">Via Francigena</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and in 1414 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-iii-deste">Niccolò III d'Este</a>, Marquis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, described it as a good ascent and a bad descent. The Vaudois crossed in 1689, and in April 1794 the French Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> under Alex Dumas defeated Piedmontese forces there, opening the way for the Army of Italy.</p><p>Napoleon I built a road over the pass to improve military connections, and by 1810 it was the most-travelled road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Mont Cenis Pass Railway, the first built on the Fell system, ran beside it from 1868 until 1871, when traffic moved to the Fréjus Rail Tunnel, which is often but wrongly called the Mont Cenis Tunnel. After the Kingdom of Sardinia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> ceded Savoy to France, the pass became a frontier, and the Italian army built Fort Cassa, Fort Varisello and Fort Roncia in 1874-1880; the Fascist government later added part of its Alpine Wall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since the Treaty of Paris of 1947 the pass and all its fortifications have lain wholly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Lac du Mont Cenis, an artificial reservoir created by a dam in 1921, covers the old road and border crossing and feeds two hydroelectric power plants. The summit has a subarctic climate, and an alpine botanical garden, the Jardin botanique de Mont Cenis, stands on the pass.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mont Cenis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The house of Savoy had ruled lands on both sides of the western Alps since the eleventh century, when Humbert the White-Handed held counties around the Lake of Geneva and the valleys leading to the <b>Mont Cenis</b> and Great St Bernard passes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The margraves controlled the routes over the <b>Mont Cenis</b> and Mont Genèvre passes, a source of wealth and strategic weight.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-turin">March of Turin</a></li></ul></section>
